# Handy Android Guardrails

These rules are derived directly from `Handy_Android_Build_Plan_v2.md`
and the v1 Cursor prompt. They are non-negotiable for v1.

## SDK targets (locked for v1, as of DL-017)

* `minSdk = 26`
* `targetSdk = 36`
* `compileSdk = 36`
* Android 16 / API 36 is the shipping target. Google Play requires
  `targetSdk >= 36` for all new apps and updates starting April 2026,
  which is the deadline we ship under. Android 16 went stable June 2025
  and ships by default on Pixel 10+.
* A compatibility smoke lane may run against Android 17 / API 37 in
  later sprints; keep the above numbers unchanged without a
  DESIGN_NOTES entry and the user's explicit approval.

## Modules

* `:core` is pure Kotlin/JVM. No `android.*` imports, no `androidx.*`
  imports, no `kotlinx.coroutines.android`, no `Context`, no
  `SharedPreferences`, no Compose. If you need a platform capability,
  define an interface in `:core` and implement it in
  `:android-runtime`.
* `:android-runtime` contains Android adapters and wrappers only. **No
  UI** (no Compose, no Activities, no Fragments, no View subclasses
  tied to a screen) **and no manifest-declared Android components**
  in v1. Every `Service`, `AccessibilityService`, `Activity`,
  `BroadcastReceiver`, or `ContentProvider` lives in `:app`.
* `:app` owns all manifest-declared components (`HandyApplication`,
  `ChatActivity`, `SettingsActivity`, `OnboardingActivity`, the
  floating-widget overlay service, the foreground service, and the
  `AccessibilityService`) **and** all Compose UI. It depends on
  `:android-runtime` and `:core`; `:android-runtime` must not depend
  on `:app`.
* A unit test in `:core` scans the compiled classpath and fails the
  build if any `android.` reference leaks in.

## Concurrency

* No main-thread I/O. All network, disk, and Accessibility tree walks
  run on `Dispatchers.IO` or `Dispatchers.Default`.
* No bitmap or JSON-heavy work on the main thread.
* Use structured concurrency. Every `CoroutineScope` you create must
  have a well-defined cancellation owner (ViewModel, Service,
  lifecycle).
* No `GlobalScope`. Ever.
* `StateFlow` for UI state. `SharedFlow` (replay=0, extraBufferCapacity
  sized intentionally) for one-shot events.
* `StrictMode` with the thread + VM policies enabled in debug builds;
  crashes in debug on main-thread disk/network and on leaked closables.

## OS resources

* Every owner of an OS resource (`SpeechRecognizer`, `TextToSpeech`,
  `MediaRecorder`, `AudioRecord`, `AccessibilityService` connections,
  `MediaProjection` sessions, window overlays) must expose
  `release()` and be released in `onDestroy()` / service teardown.
* Overlays use `WindowManager.LayoutParams.TYPE_APPLICATION_OVERLAY`.
  The idle floating widget uses
  `FLAG_NOT_FOCUSABLE | FLAG_LAYOUT_IN_SCREEN | FLAG_LAYOUT_NO_LIMITS`.
  v1 has no overlay-hosted text input — typed chat lives in
  `ChatActivity`, not an overlay.

## Persistence (v1)

* Chat history is stored as **JSON files** — not Room. One file per
  tool-memory key; atomic write via temp-file-then-rename; per-tool
  cap enforced at write time. The on-disk schema is JSON-compatible
  with the macOS `ChatMessage` / `MessageRole` / `ConversationTurn`
  schema so histories can round-trip later.
* App settings live in `DataStore<Preferences>`.
* Secrets (Claude API key, optional Brave/Jina/GitHub keys) live in
  `EncryptedSharedPreferences` backed by the Android Keystore.
* **Do not add Room in v1.** If a future feature genuinely needs
  relational storage, that is a v2 discussion.

## LLM brain

* There is exactly one `LlmClient` implementation wired in v1:
  `ClaudeLlmClient`. It uses OkHttp + Server-Sent Events.
* The Claude system prompts — `chatSystemPrompt`,
  `voiceSystemPrompt`, `tutorModeSystemPrompt`, and the dynamic
  `webSearchPromptAddendum` — live in
  `core/prompts/PromptCatalog.kt` and are copied verbatim from
  `HandyManager.swift`. See the "macOS prompt reuse" rule below
  for the full inventory and the ONLY platform adaptations that
  are allowed.
* `[POINT:…]` and `[SPOKEN]...[/SPOKEN]` markers are parsed
  by `core/parsing/AssistantMarkupParser.kt` and have unit tests
  covering every edge case from the macOS implementation
  (`PointParser.stripPointTags`, `extractSpokenPart`,
  `clampVoiceSpokenForTTS` with `maxChars = 420`,
  `clampVoiceSpokenForOverlay` with `maxChars = 110`). Only the
  **semantic** pointer form (Strategy B) is produced in v1; the
  parser accepts both shapes so legacy macOS samples still round-trip
  in tests.

## Guided / multi-step workflows

* The macOS continuous guided-workflow / multi-step runner is
  **out of scope for v1**. Do not wire a multi-step orchestrator,
  step planner, or "do the next thing" loop. v1 is one-shot
  guidance + semantic pointing + screen reading + direct intents.
* Tutor mode is ported for parity but **off by default**. Do not
  expand its behaviour beyond flag-wired parity in v1.

## macOS prompt reuse — port verbatim, adapt only when you must

The user has spent months tuning the macOS prompts, loading verbs,
status strings, error messages, and UX contracts. Treat those as
the product spec, not as starting-point suggestions. Before writing
any assistant-facing string in Android, grep `./Handy/` for the
concept. If it exists and is platform-agnostic, port it exactly.

### Verbatim ports (no changes allowed)

* `HandyManager.swift → loadingVerbs` (34 strings).
* `HandyManager.swift → webSearchStatusText` tool-name → status-line
  mapping ("Searching the web…", "Searching GitHub…", "Reading
  page…", "Looking things up…").
* `HandyManager.swift → introPrefix` behaviour (first-turn-only
  greeting prefix).
* `PointParser` utilities, including the exact `maxChars` caps
  (420 for TTS, 110 for overlay) and the sentence-boundary
  truncation rule.
* `ChatMessage`, `MessageRole`, `ConversationTurn` JSON schema —
  Android writes the same JSON that macOS reads.
* `AssistantMode`, `STTProvider`, `TTSProvider`, `SarvamVoice` enum
  raw values and display descriptions.
* `WebSearchService` citation contract: "briefly mention your source
  naturally… in voice responses, just name the source; in chat, you
  may include a link. do not list raw URLs in spoken responses."
* All user-facing error strings that are platform-agnostic (no API
  key configured, network error, rate limit, invalid response).

### Prompts — ported verbatim, with ONE narrow exception

`chatSystemPrompt`, `voiceSystemPrompt`, and
`tutorModeSystemPrompt` are copied into Kotlin string constants in
`PromptCatalog.kt` with every line preserved. The ONLY transforms
allowed are the platform adaptation rules below. Every other word,
rule, example, and stylistic choice (all lowercase, no emojis, never
say "simply" or "just", "plant a seed" closing sentence,
"don't read out code verbatim", the exemplar dialogues) is
preserved exactly.

If you believe a prompt line needs changing for reasons other than
the allowed platform adaptations, stop and ask. Do not unilaterally
"improve" the user's tuned prompts.

### Platform adaptation — the ONLY allowed transforms

Apply these mechanically; do not editorialise.

| macOS text                                           | Android text                                             |
|------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
| "on **macos**" / "on macos"                          | "on **android**" / "on android"                          |
| "menu bar" (as the home for Handy)                   | "floating widget" (as the home for Handy)                |
| "open handy's chat from the **menu bar**"            | "tap the handy widget to open the full chat"             |
| "command, option, control" shortcuts                 | gestures: "tap the widget", "long-press to speak"; omit keyboard shortcuts unless the user has a physical keyboard |
| "windows/linux" platform disclaimers                 | remove or replace with "android phones and tablets"      |
| pixel-coordinate `[POINT:x,y:label]` contract        | **semantic pointer** contract (see next bullet)          |

### Pointing contract — diverges from macOS (Strategy B, plan §8)

Android is semantic-pointer only. Update the pointing section of
each prompt to:

```
element pointing:
you have a small blue arrow that can fly to ui elements on the user's
screen. use it whenever pointing would genuinely help. append a
pointer tag at the very end of your response, AFTER your text:

  [POINT:role=<role>;text=<exact visible text>]
  or
  [POINT:viewId=<resource id suffix>]
  or
  [POINT:desc=<contentDescription>]

use the attribute you have highest confidence in. role is one of
button, link, textfield, image, checkbox, switch, tab, menuitem. the
text, viewId, or desc must match what's in the screen_text block you
were given.

if pointing wouldn't help, append [POINT:none].
```

Keep the rest of the pointing guidance (when to point, when not to,
err on the side of pointing) word-for-word. `AssistantMarkupParser`
parses both the legacy pixel form and the new semantic form; only
the semantic form is produced in v1.

### Android-only prompt addendums (NEW for Android)

Two addendums that do not exist on macOS. Wire them into
`buildSystemPrompt(mode, settings, screenSnapshot, tools)`:

1. **Screen-text addendum** (appended only when
   `ScreenInputMode` ≠ `VisionOnly` and a `ScreenTextSnapshot` is
   present):

   ```
   screen text (from accessibility): the user is in package
   {packageName}; here is the visible ui tree in flattened form. use
   this as your primary source — do not hallucinate ui elements that
   are not listed. when you point at an element, reference its text,
   contentDescription, or viewId from this block.

   <screen_ui>
   {flattened YAML-ish tree, one line per visible node}
   </screen_ui>
   ```

2. **Intent-tool addendum** (always present in v1 when intents are
   enabled):

   ```
   direct actions: for well-defined requests like "set a 10-minute
   timer", "open youtube", "call mom", "text sarah 'on my way'",
   "search google for X", prefer the `dispatch_action` tool over
   verbal instructions. ask the user for confirmation only if the
   action is destructive (calling, texting, sharing). for simple
   one-step actions, just dispatch.
   ```

The exact keyword list the router keys off (`timer`, `alarm`,
`open`, `call`, `text`, etc.) lives in
`core/intent/IntentKeywordHints.kt` and is referenced from the
prompt, not re-stated in it.

### Error-message strings — port AND relocalise platform permissions

The macOS app ships specific user-facing error strings. Port them,
but rewrite the permission-flow strings for Android settings paths.

| macOS string                                                           | Android string                                                          |
|------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| "Screen Recording permission needed. Toggle Handy OFF then ON in System Settings > Privacy & Security > Screen Recording, then relaunch." | "Screen sharing permission needed. Enable Handy in Settings > Accessibility, then try again." |
| "Speech recognition not authorized. Go to System Settings > Privacy & Security > Speech Recognition." | "Microphone permission denied. Tap the widget → Settings → grant microphone access."           |
| `ClaudeAPIError.noAPIKey` → "No Claude API key. Add one in Settings."  | same wording, points to Android settings screen                          |
| `ClaudeAPIError.httpError(429, …)` → Claude rate-limit message          | same wording, same retry guidance                                        |
| `ClaudeAPIError.networkError(…)` → "Network error: …"                   | same wording                                                             |

Anything NOT on this table is a verbatim port.

### When in doubt — grep first

Before writing a new assistant-facing string, grep for its concept
in `./Handy/`. If a similar string exists, port it and adapt only
the allowed transforms. If it genuinely doesn't exist and is not a
macOS-only concept, you may write a new one — but mention it in
your "Plan" block so the user sees it.

## Action performance (tap-for-me seam)

* v1 binds `ActionPerformer → NoopActionPerformer`.
  `NoopActionPerformer` returns `PerformResult.Unsupported` for every
  call and logs a structured breadcrumb. Do not implement a real
  performer in v1 even if it "would be easy".
* `ActionPerformer` lives in `:core` and must not reference
  `AccessibilityService`. The v2 binding will be
  `AccessibilityGestureActionPerformer` in the module that owns the
  `AccessibilityService` (`:app` in v1).

## Pointing (Strategy B only)

* Only `SemanticPointerResolver` is implemented in v1. It takes the
  assistant's pointing instruction (role + text + optional hints) and
  resolves it against the live `AccessibilityNodeInfo` tree with the
  fallback chain: exact text + role match → contentDescription match
  → viewId suffix match → fuzzy text (Levenshtein ≤ 2).
* Do **NOT** implement pixel-coordinate pointing (Strategy A)
  anywhere. Do not generate `[POINT:x,y:…]` markers. If legacy
  fixtures feed them in for parser tests, the parser tolerates them
  but the runtime must never emit them.

## Accessibility service configuration (must be declared)

The app's `AccessibilityService` XML config and runtime flags must
be configured so screen reading, window inspection, viewId-based
pointing, and screenshot capture all work. The config declares:

* `android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true"`
* `android:canTakeScreenshot="true"` (API 30+)
* `android:accessibilityEventTypes` covers at minimum
  `typeWindowStateChanged | typeWindowContentChanged`.
* `android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"`.
* A reasonable `android:notificationTimeout` (100 ms starting
  point).
* `android:canRequestFilterKeyEvents="false"` (we do not filter
  keys).

At runtime, `AccessibilityService.serviceInfo` sets:

* `AccessibilityServiceInfo.FLAG_RETRIEVE_INTERACTIVE_WINDOWS`
* `AccessibilityServiceInfo.FLAG_REPORT_VIEW_IDS`

Without the above, `getWindows()`, `viewIdResourceName`, and
`takeScreenshot` degrade silently on production devices. Do not
ship the service without verifying these flags in an
instrumentation test.

## Screen capture pipeline (v1 order)

The capture pipeline branches in this exact order:

1. **API 34+ (Android 14+)** — prefer
   `AccessibilityService.takeScreenshotOfWindow(activeWindowId, …)`
   so we capture the focused window only. Cheaper, no projection
   consent, respects per-window secure flags.
2. **API 30–33** — fall back to
   `AccessibilityService.takeScreenshot(Display.DEFAULT_DISPLAY, …)`.
3. **API 26–29** — fall back to `MediaProjection`. The
   MediaProjection-owning service declares
   `foregroundServiceType="mediaProjection"`; the pipeline registers
   a `MediaProjection.Callback`, handles `onStop()`, releases the
   virtual display + surface immediately, and clears capture state so
   no further frames are produced.

Every entry point returns a `CaptureResult` sealed type:

```
CaptureResult.Bitmap(bitmap)        // usable
CaptureResult.SecureWindow          // treated via §OS-5 below
CaptureResult.NotPermitted          // missing runtime permission or user denial
CaptureResult.Unsupported           // no backing API on this device
CaptureResult.Failed(reason)        // everything else, with a loggable reason
```

**No raw `Bitmap` crosses an orchestration boundary.** The LLM path
accepts `CaptureResult`, not `Bitmap`.

## SpeechRecognizer / STT rules

* Prefer on-device: on API 31+, use
  `SpeechRecognizer.createOnDeviceSpeechRecognizer(context)` when
  `isOnDeviceRecognitionAvailable(context)` returns true; otherwise
  use `SpeechRecognizer.createSpeechRecognizer(context)`.
* `EXTRA_PREFER_OFFLINE` is a **hint**, not the design. It may be
  set on the `RecognizerIntent`, but correctness does not depend on
  the OS honouring it.
* **All `SpeechRecognizer` calls run on the main thread.** Never
  invoke `startListening` / `stopListening` / `cancel` / `destroy`
  from `Dispatchers.IO`. The voice controller confines itself to
  `Dispatchers.Main.immediate`.
* Always call `destroy()` on teardown. Leaked recognizers keep the
  microphone and the mic indicator.
* The app manifest must declare a `<queries>` block listing
  `android.speech.RecognitionService` so the OS-provided recognizer
  resolves on targetSdk 30+.
* Voice UX must support partial results, graceful release-to-submit
  (empty release → no-op, never a stuck spinner), and a friendly
  fallback when on-device recognition is unavailable. Every commit
  that touches STT ships with at least one test.

## Package visibility / launchable-app resolution

* Do **not** do naive fuzzy `PackageManager` lookups on every chat
  request.
* Build and cache a `LaunchableAppIndex` at app start from
  `PackageManager.queryIntentActivities(Intent(ACTION_MAIN).addCategory(CATEGORY_LAUNCHER), …)`.
* Refresh the index on `ACTION_PACKAGE_ADDED` /
  `ACTION_PACKAGE_REMOVED` / `ACTION_PACKAGE_REPLACED` broadcasts
  (registered dynamically to satisfy targetSdk 35 restrictions).
* Respect package visibility: keep the manifest `<queries>` minimal
  and explicit. If you need a query, list it; don't use the
  `QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES` permission.

## Intent dispatch contract

* `dispatch_action` is the **only** action-tool registered with the
  LLM. The dispatcher never fires an action without the LLM
  explicitly calling that tool — no regex-based inference from free
  text.
* Every dispatch returns a structured `IntentResult`:

  ```
  sealed class IntentResult {
      data class Dispatched(val component: ComponentName?) : IntentResult()
      data object ChooserShown : IntentResult()
      data class NeedsConfirmation(val reason: String) : IntentResult()
      data object NoHandler : IntentResult()
      data class Failed(val reason: String) : IntentResult()
  }
  ```

* Destructive actions (placing a call, sending a message, sharing
  content) return `NeedsConfirmation` and require explicit user
  approval before dispatch. Non-destructive one-step actions
  (timer, alarm, open URL, open app, maps search) may dispatch
  directly.

## Web search

* Web search is wired end-to-end but `webSearchEnabled` defaults to
  `false`. Respect the toggle — do not make calls to Brave/Jina/GitHub
  when it's off, and do not send search-tool definitions to Claude in
  that mode.

## Voice + animations

* Voice capture and TTS are "must-work" features. Every commit that
  touches audio ships with an instrumentation test that covers at
  least one round-trip. TTS speaks only the clamped
  `[SPOKEN]…[/SPOKEN]` segment (420 chars for TTS, 110 for overlay).
* Animations are parity with macOS. Match the curves and durations
  specified in build plan §12 exactly. If you change a curve,
  document the reason in DESIGN_NOTES.md.

## UI

* Theme is Apple-class. Inter font family, the typography scale in
  plan §11, the colour tokens in plan §11, the motion tokens in
  plan §11. Do not introduce new design tokens without a DESIGN_NOTES
  entry.
* Dark theme is default. Light theme must remain usable (contrast
  ratios ≥ AA).
* Haptics: `HapticFeedbackConstants.LONG_PRESS` on voice arm,
  `HapticFeedbackConstants.CONTEXT_CLICK` on snap.

## Dependencies

* Do not add a new dependency without writing its name + justification
  in DESIGN_NOTES.md "Dependencies" section.
* No networking libraries other than OkHttp. No Retrofit unless the
  plan explicitly adds it.
* No "AI" helper libraries. Claude is called over raw SSE.

## Play / policy deliverables (treat as must-ship, not later-risk)

v1 does not ship until these are in place:

* A prominent **in-app disclosure** for Accessibility Service use on
  first launch, stating in plain language what Handy will read on
  screen and what it will not, with a link to the privacy policy.
* An **explicit consent step** before the Accessibility setting
  deep-link is opened; the user must be able to decline and use a
  reduced mode that leaves the toggle off.
* A drafted **Play Console Accessibility / permissions
  declaration** matching actual runtime behaviour (no tap-for-me in
  v1; clearly describe read + pointing + intent dispatch).
* A short **review artifact list**: screen-recorded demo of the
  consent flow, of a canonical chat + pointing session, and of the
  Accessibility-off fallback.
* A **privacy policy** whose wording matches the app's actual
  behaviour (what leaves the device, what doesn't, retention,
  third-party keys).
* Avoid marketing copy that positions Handy as an unrestricted
  autonomous controller of the phone.

## Android OS constraints (v1 must-handle — do NOT skip any of these)

These are the five platform traps that will silently break v1 if you
are not deliberate about them. They are not "nice to haves" — each
one will crash the app or produce garbage output on real devices
until handled. Wire them into the relevant classes from day one;
don't defer.

### OS-1. Foreground Service types (manifest crash + Android 15 start rules)

Starting on Android 14 (API 34), starting a foreground service
without declaring an explicit `foregroundServiceType` in the
manifest throws a fatal `MissingForegroundServiceTypeException` and
crashes the app on first launch. Because `targetSdk 35` is required,
this rule applies to every foreground service in Handy, with the
added Android 15 wrinkle that background-initiated FGS starts are
more restricted than before.

Rules:

* `AssistantForegroundService` (the widget/lifecycle anchor, which
  also owns the microphone during voice capture) must declare
  `android:foregroundServiceType="specialUse|microphone"` in
  `AndroidManifest.xml`.
* The manifest must include both
  `android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SPECIAL_USE` and
  `android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MICROPHONE` alongside the
  base `FOREGROUND_SERVICE` permission.
* Any service that uses `MediaProjection` (the screen-capture
  fallback path) must additionally declare
  `android:foregroundServiceType="mediaProjection"` on its own
  `<service>` entry and request
  `android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MEDIA_PROJECTION`.
* When declaring `specialUse`, also include the
  `<property android:name="android.app.PROPERTY_SPECIAL_USE_FGS_SUBTYPE" .../>`
  element with a short, accurate justification string (e.g.,
  `"on-screen AI assistant overlay that must remain visible across
  apps"`).
* `Service.startForeground(...)` must be called within 5 seconds of
  `onStartCommand()` with a notification whose channel is created at
  application start — not lazily.
* **Android 15 `SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW` background-start exemption:**
  the overlay must **already be visible** at the moment the FGS is
  started if we rely on this exemption. If the widget is not
  currently attached to `WindowManager`, route the start through an
  activity or a user-initiated broadcast rather than assuming the
  exemption covers us.

### OS-2. Idle-widget overlay stays non-focusable; typed chat lives in `ChatActivity`

In v1, the floating widget is the **only** overlay that hosts
interactive chrome, and the idle widget uses
`FLAG_NOT_FOCUSABLE` so it does not steal touches from the app
behind it. **There is no chat-panel overlay in v1.** A tap on the
widget launches `ChatActivity` (its own window, normal IME
handling), so the historical "remove `FLAG_NOT_FOCUSABLE` when the
chat panel opens" dance does not apply.

Rules:

* Treat the overlay's `LayoutParams.flags` as state through a single
  function `overlayFlagsFor(mode: OverlayMode): Int` in the
  floating-widget overlay service. In v1, `OverlayMode` has a single
  public value (`IdleWidget`) that returns
  `FLAG_NOT_FOCUSABLE | FLAG_LAYOUT_IN_SCREEN | FLAG_LAYOUT_NO_LIMITS`.
  The helper exists so v2 can add another mode without refactoring.
* When the user taps the widget, start `ChatActivity` via an
  explicit `Intent` with `FLAG_ACTIVITY_NEW_TASK`. The widget
  overlay is hidden while `ChatActivity` is foreground and restored
  on close.
* An instrumentation test must assert that typing into the
  `ChatActivity` input actually commits characters — don't trust
  manual testing for IME behaviour.
* **Do not add a `ChatPanelOverlayService`** or any other overlay
  that hosts a text field. If a future product decision needs one,
  it ships in a later version and brings its own full IME test
  matrix.

### OS-3. Screenshot API tiers + MediaProjection lifecycle

Because `minSdk 26`, a single capture call site cannot assume any
particular screenshot API. The capture pipeline must branch in the
exact order below.

Rules:

* `AccessibilityScreenReader` and any other capture site route
  through the central pipeline:
  1. **API 34+ (Android 14+)** — prefer
     `AccessibilityService.takeScreenshotOfWindow(activeWindowId, …)`.
     Cheaper, respects per-window secure flags, no projection
     consent.
  2. **API 30–33** — use
     `AccessibilityService.takeScreenshot(Display.DEFAULT_DISPLAY, …)`.
  3. **API 26–29** — use `MediaProjection` fallback.
* Both accessibility-based calls are guarded by `@RequiresApi` on
  the private method that actually calls them, with an SDK-agnostic
  public entry point.
* The capture abstraction returns the `CaptureResult` sealed type;
  the orchestrator handles each variant explicitly. Never silently
  degrade to "no screenshot".
* **MediaProjection lifecycle discipline:** register a
  `MediaProjection.Callback`; in `onStop()` (or when capture is
  cancelled), immediately release the `VirtualDisplay` and the
  backing `Surface`/`ImageReader`, and clear any cached capture
  state so no further frames are produced. Leaking the virtual
  display keeps sending black frames to the LLM long after the
  user stopped the session.
* A unit test must verify the API branch picks the right path on
  simulated `Build.VERSION.SDK_INT = 28`, `= 31`, and `= 34`.

### OS-4. Compose inside a WindowManager overlay (lifecycle wiring)

Jetpack Compose expects to live inside an `Activity` that provides
a `LifecycleOwner`, a `ViewModelStoreOwner`, and a
`SavedStateRegistryOwner`. Inflating a `ComposeView` directly into
`WindowManager` from a `Service` will crash at `setContent { }`
with "ViewTreeLifecycleOwner not found" (or the
`SavedStateRegistryOwner` equivalent). In v1 this applies to the
floating widget overlay (and any future pointer overlay chrome).

Rules:

* Every overlay `ComposeView` must be hosted by a small helper
  class — `OverlayComposeHost` — that:
  1. Owns a `LifecycleRegistry`.
  2. Owns a `SavedStateRegistryController` (with
     `performAttach()` + `performRestore(null)`).
  3. Optionally owns a `ViewModelStore` if the overlay has
     ViewModels.
  4. Calls, before `setContent { }`:
     ```kotlin
     root.setViewTreeLifecycleOwner(this)
     root.setViewTreeSavedStateRegistryOwner(this)
     root.setViewTreeViewModelStoreOwner(this)
     ```
  5. Moves the lifecycle `Lifecycle.State.CREATED` →
     `STARTED` → `RESUMED` as the overlay is attached, and
     `RESUMED` → `STARTED` → `CREATED` → `DESTROYED` on tear-down.
* The host's `release()` must move to `DESTROYED` before
  `windowManager.removeView(root)` to avoid leaked compositions.
* Do not shortcut this with reflection or by copying a random
  Gist — write it once in `OverlayComposeHost.kt` and reuse it
  for every overlay.

### OS-5. Secure / unusable capture — don't feed blank frames to the LLM

Banking apps, password managers, DRM'd video, and incognito browser
tabs set `FLAG_SECURE` on their windows. Both
`AccessibilityService.takeScreenshot(…)` and `MediaProjection`
return all-black buffers for those surfaces. Sending a black image
to Claude produces confident hallucinated answers, which is worse
than a clear error.

Rules:

* The capture pipeline returns a `CaptureResult` sealed type:
  `Bitmap`, `SecureWindow`, `NotPermitted`, `Unsupported`,
  `Failed(reason)`. The LLM path never accepts raw bitmaps; it
  accepts `CaptureResult`.
* **Primary detection (required)** comes from the capture API
  itself: official `takeScreenshot` / `takeScreenshotOfWindow`
  failure codes such as `ERROR_TAKE_SCREENSHOT_NO_ACCESSIBILITY_ACCESS`
  and `ERROR_TAKE_SCREENSHOT_INTERNAL_ERROR`, and — for
  MediaProjection — a cheap "is the bitmap essentially all-black"
  sampler (16-pixel stride; >99% of samples within 3 of RGB(0,0,0)
  → classify as secure / unusable).
* `windowInfo.isSecure` from `AccessibilityService.getWindows()` is
  **not a required source of truth** and must not be the only
  signal. Treat it as an optional hint: if the capture API did not
  already classify the frame as secure, a `true` value may promote
  a `Bitmap` result to `SecureWindow`, but a `false` value never
  overrides a secure classification from the capture path.
* On `SecureWindow`, do not call the LLM with an image. Instead,
  inject a system-style chat message into the conversation:
  `"I can't see this screen — the app is marked secure (e.g. banking,
  incognito, or password manager). Ask me again from a non-secure
  screen, or paste the text you want help with."`
* The accessibility text-reading path (`readScreenText`) consumes
  the same pipeline: if the capture channel flags the active
  window as secure, or if a tree walk returns no usable text while
  the capture path reports `SecureWindow`, return the same
  injected system message rather than a possibly-partial node
  list.

## Forbidden

* `!!` (not-null assertion) outside of test code.
* `runBlocking` outside of tests.
* `LiveData` (use `StateFlow`).
* `View` XML layouts (Compose only).
* `System.out` / `println` for logging (use `Timber`, planted per
  build type).
* `@SuppressLint` without a comment explaining why.
* Disabling lint, ktlint, or detekt rules without a DESIGN_NOTES
  entry.
* Starting a foreground service without `foregroundServiceType` in
  the manifest (see OS-1).
* Any new overlay window that hosts a text input (v1 chat is
  `ChatActivity` only — see OS-2).
* Calling `AccessibilityService.takeScreenshot(…)` or
  `takeScreenshotOfWindow(…)` without the tiered API guard
  (see OS-3).
* Inflating a `ComposeView` into `WindowManager` without an
  `OverlayComposeHost` (see OS-4).
* Passing a raw `Bitmap` to `LlmClient` — only `CaptureResult`
  crosses that boundary (see OS-5).
* Adding Room to the v1 build for chat history (see "Persistence").
* Generating `[POINT:x,y:…]` pixel pointers at runtime (Strategy A
  is out of v1).
* Wiring a real `ActionPerformer` in v1 — the binding is
  `NoopActionPerformer` until v2.
* Implementing a multi-step / continuous guided workflow in v1.

---

## V2 extensions

These rules apply to V2 work (Phase 0 onward per the V2 Execution
Phases section in `Handy_Android_Cursor_Prompt_v2.md`). They build on
the V1 rules above — they do not replace them. The authoritative V2
spec is [`Handy_Android_Build_Plan_V2_Scope.md`](../../Handy_Android_Build_Plan_V2_Scope.md);
if this section and the scope doc disagree, the scope doc wins.

### Overlay chat panel (V2 primary quick surface — scope §2)

* The overlay chat panel is the **default** entry for a widget tap.
  `ChatActivity` opens only on explicit user expand or from settings /
  diagnostics. Do not auto-launch `ChatActivity` from background.
* `OverlayMode` gains a `ChatPanel` value in V2.
  `overlayFlagsFor(OverlayMode.ChatPanel)` returns
  `FLAG_NOT_TOUCH_MODAL | FLAG_LAYOUT_IN_SCREEN |
  FLAG_WATCH_OUTSIDE_TOUCH` with `softInputMode =
  SOFT_INPUT_ADJUST_PAN`. The idle flag set is unchanged.
* The panel drops `FLAG_NOT_FOCUSABLE` while open and restores the
  idle flag set on close via the same `overlayFlagsFor(mode)` helper
  (OS-2 is preserved).
* Panel open / close must not leak `ComposeView`, `ViewModelStore`, or
  `WindowManager` resources. Reuse `OverlayComposeHost`.
* Panel must handle: IME focus transitions, orientation change,
  split-screen / multi-window, overlay permission revocation,
  app-switch while open, animation interruption, Accessibility-off
  reduced mode.
* Panel state is a single `StateFlow<OverlayPanelState>` with the
  values from scope §2 (`Hidden`, `WidgetIdle`, `WidgetListening`,
  `WidgetThinking`, `ChatPanelCollapsed`, `ChatPanelExpanded`,
  `PanelStreaming`, `PanelActionConfirming`).

### Unified Buddy (widget = pointer — scope §3)

* V2 does **NOT** introduce a second overlay window for pointing.
  There is no `PointerOverlayController`, no `CompanionCursorManager`
  port. The floating widget *is* the pointer.
* The widget is one Compose overlay that morphs between Docked,
  Listening, Thinking, Flying, Pointing, Acting, and Speaking states
  per the scope §3 state machine. Visual continuity is the point —
  the user watches one object the whole time.
* Flight math: Bezier with
  `duration = clamp(distance / 800.0, 0.6, 1.4)` s,
  `arcHeight = min(distance * 0.2, 80)` px, smoothstep easing
  `t' = t² · (3 − 2t)`, dwell uniform random in `[3.0, 5.0]` s,
  triangle-tangent rotation, flight-in `DecelerateInterpolator(2f)`,
  landed pulse `OvershootInterpolator(1.5f)` `1.0 → 1.14` 600 ms
  reverse forever (cancelled on return). Do not invent new curves.
* The visual is the seven-layer Glass Lens (scope §15 recipe #1).
  Same render in Docked and Flying; only overlaid chrome changes.

### Bubble taxonomy (scope §3)

* Four colors only: yellow (transcript) / teal (action-in-progress) /
  green (assistant response) / blue (pointer navigation label).
* Only one bubble is visible at a time. Mutual-exclusion rules:
  green > blue; teal > green; yellow fades when processing starts.
* Teal is a **passive status indicator**, not interactive.
  Confirmation chips live inside the overlay chat panel
  (`PanelActionConfirming` state), never on the lens.
* Bubbles are clamped: green 110 chars (`clampVoiceSpokenForOverlay`),
  blue 30 chars, teal ≤ 30 chars.
* Bubbles render with the glassmorphism palette from scope §15
  recipe #8. No `FLAG_BLUR_BEHIND`.

### Tap-for-me (scope §4)

* V2 Hilt binding is
  `ActionPerformer → AccessibilityGestureActionPerformer` when
  `settings.tapForMeEnabled = true`; otherwise
  `NoopActionPerformer` remains. The flip is per-setting, not a
  permanent swap.
* Allowed actions: tap visible semantic node, long-press visible
  semantic node, scroll one viewport, explicit directional swipe,
  open notification shade / Quick Settings, activate clearly
  labelled buttons / tabs / menu items / toggles in the current app.
* Forbidden: open-ended automation, repeated retries until success,
  hidden UI traversal, arbitrary text entry via `ACTION_SET_TEXT`,
  multi-step chaining, cross-app plans without explicit
  confirmation, anything autonomous.
* **Node-first, gesture-second** (scope §15 recipe #3): after
  `SemanticPointerResolver.resolve` returns an
  `AccessibilityNodeInfo`, if `node.isClickable` call
  `node.performAction(ACTION_CLICK)`; only fall back to
  `dispatchGesture` at the node's bounds center when not clickable.
  Long-press mirrors (`ACTION_LONG_CLICK` first; gesture fallback).
* **Cache-at-tap snapshot** (scope §15 recipe #4): the overlay
  presenter caches `(packageName, umbrellaSite,
  accessibilityMarksSnapshot, captureHint, timestampEpochMs)` at
  the instant the widget is tapped or long-pressed. The panel and
  voice pipeline consume the cached snapshot; they do not re-read
  `rootInActiveWindow` inside a focused panel.
* Confirmation policy (scope §4.1): no confirmation for benign
  one-step actions in the current foreground app; required for
  cross-app, send/share/post/call/text/email, settings with
  device-wide impact, notification reply/dismiss, purchase/submit
  flows, and any borderline-confidence target.
* Action audit (scope §4.3): every performed tap-for-me or
  `dispatch_action` writes an `AuditEvent` to
  `:android-runtime/audit/AuditStore`. Oldest entries prune on
  write. Last 20 entries surface in `DiagnosticsActivity`.
* Do not retry on failure. Re-resolve a stale node exactly once,
  then fail. Low-quality WebView tree → fall back to pointer, not
  brute-force gesture.

### BrainRouter + LocalGenAiClient (scope §5)

* `LlmClient` is unchanged. V2 adds `LocalGenAiClient`
  (`:core/llm/LocalGenAiClient.kt`) with the exact shape in scope
  §5.1.
* `BrainRouter` (`:core/brain/BrainRouter.kt`) is the single entry
  point for picking a provider per request. Default routing order:
  Claude cloud → Gemini cloud (if selected or Claude unreachable)
  → Gemini Nano (only if task is in the `LocalTask` whitelist AND
  `LocalAvailability.Available` AND user setting allows).
  Otherwise `BrainDecision.FailGracefully`.
* Local-preferred mode (`settings.preferLocalWhenPossible = true`):
  eligible text-only tasks go local first; non-eligible fall
  through to cloud.
* **`LocalTask` allowed whitelist (scope §5.3):**
  `SUMMARIZE_TEXT`, `REWRITE_TEXT`, `TRANSLATE_TEXT`,
  `SCREEN_TEXT_QA`, `NOTIFICATION_SUMMARY`, `CLIPBOARD_TRANSFORM`,
  `SHORT_TITLE_OR_LABEL`.
* **Forbidden for local AI in V2, ever:** web search, image
  grounding from screenshots, tool planning, action selection,
  cross-app automation, open-ended long-context chat, multi-turn
  memory-heavy sessions.
* Local outputs **cannot** directly trigger action tools. They go
  through the same post-validation / moderation / truncation as
  cloud outputs.
* Gemini cloud ships with an "experimental" tag in Settings and
  remains **not default** until parity tests (same fixture set as
  Claude) are green.
* Never silently pretend local AI is equivalent to cloud. Label
  local mode in `DiagnosticsActivity`.

### RequestBudgeter + AccessibilityMarksProvider (scope §6)

* Every request routes through `RequestBudgeter.budget(message,
  mode)` before calling `LlmClient`. The returned `BudgetedRequest`
  picks one of: `TEXT_ONLY`, `FOCUSED_WINDOW`, `CURRENT_DISPLAY`,
  `BROADER`, `NONE`.
* Never send a `SecureWindow`, `NotPermitted`, `Unsupported`, or
  `Failed` frame to the model (OS-5 is preserved).
* When the panel is open and the target window is known, prefer
  `takeScreenshotOfWindow(activeWindowId)` over
  `takeScreenshot(DEFAULT_DISPLAY)`. Broader capture is the
  exception, not the default.
* `AccessibilityMarksProvider` emits the compact JSON shape from
  scope §15 recipe #2 (≤ 50 nodes, `isInteresting` filter, password
  fields flagged with `password: true` and no `text`). Marks are
  cheap; include them whenever available.

### Semantic pointing from screen-text / marks path (scope §7)

* Strategy B stays the only pointing strategy. Runtime never emits
  `[POINT:x,y:label]`.
* Semantic `[POINT]` must resolve from vision path, screen-text
  path, and accessibility-marks path. Resolver fallback chain is
  text+role → `contentDescription` → `viewId` suffix → fuzzy
  (Levenshtein ≤ 2) → fail.
* If marks provided a viewId hint, the resolver starts at the
  viewId step.

### Notification listener (scope §8)

* `HandyNotificationListenerService` is opt-in via onboarding
  disclosure + settings toggle. Disabled by default.
* V2 features: summarize recent notifications, read top
  notification text safely, open source app, filter by package,
  group by app / conversation.
* Guarded V2 features (require confirmation + audit): explicit
  dismiss, explicit `RemoteInput` reply.
* Forbidden in V2: ambient autonomous triage, auto-reply-while-away,
  rule engines.
* Handle: permission revoked while running, work-profile
  notifications, redacted lock-screen / private notifications,
  grouped notifications, media / ongoing, bubble notifications,
  stale keys, custom remote views.

### Clipboard assist (scope §9)

* Access is tied to **visible Handy state or explicit user request
  only**. No ambient harvesting.
* V2 features: summarize / rewrite / translate / explain copied
  text; share transformed text back; optional clipboard hint chip
  while Handy is visible.
* Rules: dedupe by hash, size-cap 32 KB, conservative detection of
  password / OTP-shaped clips (skip auto-processing), mark
  sensitive transforms via `EXTRA_IS_SENSITIVE` (API 33+).
* Respect background-clipboard restrictions on Android 10+.
* Do not read the clipboard from a background service when Handy
  UI is not visible.

### DiagnosticsActivity (scope §10)

* New read-only activity in `:app`. Compose `LazyColumn`.
* Required fields: overlay permission state, accessibility
  `StateFlow<AccessibilityConnectionState>`, notification access
  state, microphone permission, speech recognizer availability,
  on-device speech availability, current foreground app / package /
  tool, current browser / site umbrella label, current capture
  mode, last `CaptureResult`, secure-screen state, current
  provider, local GenAI availability + capability list, last model
  error, last tool call, last `AuditEvent`, recent audit tail
  (20 events).

### Quick Settings tile + Assist entry (scope §11)

* `HandyQuickSettingsTileService` in `:app` with a configurable
  action (open panel / start voice / open full chat). Use
  `TileService.requestAddTileService` on API 33+.
* Launcher icon opens `ChatActivity` directly (not onboarding after
  first launch). Adaptive + themed + monochrome icons.
* Optional Assist entry (`HandyAssistIntentService`) is off by
  default. Must degrade gracefully on OEMs where
  `VoiceInteractionService` is inconsistent. **Never the only way
  to access Handy.**

### Tutor mode V2 (scope §12)

* Opt-in; off by default for new users.
* Can observe context, explain visible UI, give proactive nudges
  after idle, point, use screen-text path, use `LocalGenAiClient`
  for summarization.
* **Must NOT** run a continuous runner, autonomously click the UI,
  create open-ended loops.
* Guardrails: minimum 60 s between nudges, per-app cooldowns,
  suspend under battery-saver / thermal throttling, stop on app
  switch or user interrupt, clear disclosure in settings.

### Borrowings from `cursorbuddy-android-main` (scope §15)

* Port **recipes, techniques, numeric constants — not Kotlin source
  lines**. Every borrowing is a clean-room reimplementation in our
  own Compose / adapter code. AGPL-3.0 boundary in `DESIGN_NOTES.md`.
* The ten numbered recipes in scope §15 are the authoritative
  contract:

  1. Seven-layer Glass Lens render (`:app/widget/LensRenderer.kt`).
  2. Compact Accessibility-marks JSON + `isInteresting` filter
     (`:android-runtime/accessibility/AccessibilityMarksProvider.kt`).
  3. Node-first, gesture-second tap
     (`:app/accessibility/AccessibilityGestureActionPerformer.kt`).
  4. Cache-at-tap snapshot (overlay presenter).
  5. Overlay chat panel IME choreography.
  6. Voice auto-submit 300 ms grace (`:app/voice/VoiceController.kt`).
  7. `flagIncludeNotImportantViews` on the Accessibility service.
  8. Glassmorphism palette + no `FLAG_BLUR_BEHIND`.
  9. Per-app quick-prompt chips (`:core/prompts/QuickPromptCatalog.kt`
     backed by `LaunchableAppIndex`).
  10. `StateFlow<AccessibilityConnectionState>` replaces polling.

* When a rule in this file and scope §15 disagree on numeric values
  or field names, the scope doc wins.

### Anti-patterns observed in `cursorbuddy-android-main` — forbidden

These are concrete anti-examples. If code starts to look like any
of them, stop and revert.

1. **Multi-step `TutorialEngine` runner with pause / resume / next /
   prev / replay controls** (reference:
   [`cursorbuddy-android-main/app/src/main/java/com/cursorbuddy/android/tutorial/TutorialEngine.kt`](../../cursorbuddy-android-main/app/src/main/java/com/cursorbuddy/android/tutorial/TutorialEngine.kt),
   [`.../overlay/TutorialControls.kt`](../../cursorbuddy-android-main/app/src/main/java/com/cursorbuddy/android/overlay/TutorialControls.kt)).
   This IS the V3 product. Not in V2.
2. **Strategy A pixel-bounds pointing from the LLM**
   ([`ClaudeAIPlanner.kt`](../../cursorbuddy-android-main/app/src/main/java/com/cursorbuddy/android/service/ClaudeAIPlanner.kt)
   — LLM emits `bounds: [l, t, r, b]`). Runtime never consumes
   pixel-coordinate `[POINT]`. Strategy B only.
3. **Orphaned `FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MEDIA_PROJECTION` permission**
   (cursorbuddy declares the permission but no service of type
   `mediaProjection`, so their capture fails on API 34+).
   OS-1 rule prevents this — this is the anti-example.
4. **20× 500 ms polling for accessibility-service readiness**
   ([`CursorBuddyService.onStartCommand` L62–73](../../cursorbuddy-android-main/app/src/main/java/com/cursorbuddy/android/service/CursorBuddyService.kt)).
   Use `StateFlow<AccessibilityConnectionState>` instead.
5. **Raw View + XML UI with a monolithic `OverlayManager`
   handing parents to child views**. We stay Compose-first.
   Port Paint recipes, not View hierarchies.

### V3 fence (must NOT be built in V2 — scope §16)

* Continuous multi-step workflow runner. No `WorkflowRunner` port
  from macOS. No `TutorialEngine`-style runner from cursorbuddy.
* Autonomous initiate-plan-execute loops.
* Arbitrary text entry via accessibility into other apps
  (broad `ACTION_SET_TEXT`).
* Ambient notification auto-triage / auto-reply / auto-dismiss rule
  engines.
* Ambient clipboard surveillance / always-on harvesting.
* Wear OS / Android Auto.
* On-device image reasoning via Gemini Nano (V2 local AI is
  text-first only).

### V2 expansions to the Forbidden list

In addition to the V1 Forbidden list above, V2 also forbids:

* Generating or consuming `[POINT:x,y:label]` at runtime (already
  V1-forbidden; reinforced).
* Introducing a second overlay window for pointing (no
  `PointerOverlayController`).
* `RenderEffect.createBlurEffect` on the overlay panel window or
  `FLAG_BLUR_BEHIND` on any Handy overlay window (glass is a
  surface property — scope §15 recipe #8).
* `QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES` permission (use `LaunchableAppIndex` +
  explicit `<queries>` only).
* Polling for `AccessibilityService` readiness. Observe
  `StateFlow<AccessibilityConnectionState>`.
* Writing to clipboard or reading from it when Handy is not
  user-visible.
* Ambient reads in `NotificationListenerService.onNotificationPosted`
  beyond the narrow V2 features in scope §8.
* Calling `node.performAction(ACTION_SET_TEXT, …)` on a node we do
  not own (V2 scope fence on arbitrary text entry).
* Binding a real `ActionPerformer` without a `settings`-gated toggle
  and an audit-store hook.
* Introducing a multi-step runner under any naming (workflow, plan,
  guide, tutorial, journey, etc.).
* Porting V2 code from `cursorbuddy-android-main` by copy-paste.
  Recipes only. See scope §15 and `DESIGN_NOTES.md`.
